98:Subsequently, to better understand the relative importance of each predictor (in this case, neurotransmitter receptor/transporter pro /uniFB01 le) in contributing to the model ' s overall predictive power, we conducted a dominance analysis for each multilinear model. Dominance analysis aids in determining how each predictor in /uniFB02 uences the model ' s /uniFB01 t by considering all possible predictor combinations within the model [82]. This involves applying the multilinear model to every potential subset of predictors, resulting in 2 n -1 subset models for a model with n predictors. In our study, we utilized the total dominance to assess each predictor ' s relative signi /uniFB01 cance [29, 82]. Total dominance is the mean increase in explained variance (R 2 ) contributed by each predictor across all subset models in which it is included [82, 83].
158:Fig. 4 Association between gray matter morphological abnormalities of the identi /uniFB01 ed subtypes and distribution of neurotransmitter receptors/transporters. A Multilinear models are constructed to analyze the association between neurotransmitter receptors/transporters and gray matter morphological abnormalities of the identi /uniFB01 ed subtypes. The bar plot displays the corresponding model goodness-of/uniFB01 t (adjusted R 2 ), with all FDR-corrected permutation p -values being less than 0.001. B Permutation testing results. C Dominance analysis is employed to determine the relative importance of predictors for each multilinear model. Total dominance values, indicative of the predictors ' relative importance, are depicted.
172:Gray matter volume is assessed with the Computational Anatomy Toolbox (CAT12, http://dbm.neuro.uni-jena.de/cat12/). The normative model is freely available (GAUSSIAN PROCESS REGRESSION AND CLASSIFICATION Toolbox version 4.2, http://www.GaussianProcess.org/gpml/code). Group-level differences in GMV are obtained using SPM12 (http://www. /uniFB01 l.ion.ucl.ac.uk/spm). Dominance analysis is performed with python toolbox (https://github.com/kaladharprajapati/ dominance\_analysis).
258:82. Budescu, David V. Dominance analysis: a new approach to the problem of relative importance of predictors in multiple regression. Psychol Bull. 1993;114:542 -51.
